INT. CATERING VAN - PARKED OUTSIDE A WEDDING VENUE - DAY

A white panel van with a three-tier cake lashed to the floor in a plexiglass box. Crumb-dusted dashboard. A wilted bouquet in a cup holder. The engine ticks as it cools.

MIRKO DELL'ORTO (40s, pastry chef, chef's whites unbuttoned) sits in the driver's seat gripping the wheel. Beside him, his assistant TAMSIN OKPARA (20s, hair net, rubber gloves), holds a clipboard against her knees. In the back, wedged between trays of sugar flowers, sit the groom's mother, BRIGITTE LACROIX-HAYES (60s, mother-of-the-groom, fascinator tilted), and her husband LEONARD HAYES (60s, brown suit, hands clasped over his belly).

Nobody speaks.

MIRKO
(finally)
It's a very nice cake.

BRIGITTE
It's leaning.

MIRKO
It is a Tower of Pisa style.

BRIGITTE
It's a tower of Pisa, Mirko, in the sense that it's falling over.

LEONARD
Brigitte.

BRIGITTE
Don't "Brigitte" me.

TAMSIN
Chef, I can fix it with a toothpick.

MIRKO
Tamsin.

TAMSIN
Three toothpicks and a wad of buttercream.

MIRKO
No toothpicks.

TAMSIN
A skewer, then.

MIRKO
Tamsin, no.

She puts down the clipboard. She reaches over the seat, pulls a long bamboo skewer from a drawer, and gives it a look.

BRIGITTE
What's she doing?

LEONARD
She's building a cake.

BRIGITTE
It's already built.

LEONARD
It's been built wrongly.

BRIGITTE
Leonard, do you ever listen to yourself?

LEONARD
Every morning, dear.

Mirko turns around in his seat. He regards the cake the way a general regards a failing front.

MIRKO
Okay. We do this in five minutes. Tamsin, the skewer. Leonard, hold the top tier.

LEONARD
Me?

MIRKO
You have steady hands.

LEONARD
I have a tremor.

MIRKO
Then use the tremor.

BRIGITTE
Mirko, I swear to God.

MIRKO
Madame, I have never lost a cake.

BRIGITTE
You are losing this one.

MIRKO
I am not losing it. I am fixing it.

He climbs into the back. The van rocks. Leonard, uncertain, reaches out. Tamsin kneels. The skewer slides in at an angle, slowly, and the top tier begins to straighten, one grudging degree at a time.

LEONARD
It's moving.

TAMSIN
It's working.

BRIGITTE
(under her breath)
Mother of God.

MIRKO
Hold still.

LEONARD
I am still.

MIRKO
You're breathing.

LEONARD
It's a vital function.

The tier snaps into place. A little rain of sugar pearls patters to the floor. All four of them freeze, hands in the air, as though a bird has landed on them.

BRIGITTE
Well.

MIRKO
Well.

TAMSIN
It's straight.

LEONARD
It's straight.

BRIGITTE
(quietly)
Thank you, Mirko.

MIRKO
Madame.

He nods. He climbs back out. Brigitte straightens her fascinator. Leonard wipes his palms on his trousers. Tamsin sits back, flushed, still holding the skewer like a baton.

The back door of the van swings open. A bright, noisy hall beyond. A clipboard bangs twice on the door frame, and a bored voice somewhere says the cake team is up.

MIRKO
We're ready.

He looks at the cake. It leans, very slightly, again.

MIRKO (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Don't breathe.
